{"title":"2017 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 18","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2:_Similar_triangles_with_Pythagorean\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2: Similar triangles with Pythagorean</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2b:_Area_shortcut\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2b: Area shortcut</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3:_Similar_triangles_without_Pythagorean\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3: Similar triangles without Pythagorean</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Coordinate_Geometry)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Coordinate Geometry)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(No_sqrts)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (No sqrts)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_6:_Trigonometry_&amp;_Polar_Coordinates\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6: Trigonometry &amp; Polar Coordinates</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn_(Similar_Triangles)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn (Similar Triangles)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The diameter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> of a circle of radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is extended to a point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> outside the circle so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/f/87f7d95c4cd037f3050ac44ab28c2e112bb989f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD=3$\" width=\"63\" height=\"12\" />. Point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is chosen so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/d/cbdfd511c441a7fa2da73ee9f74ba04d8a88239a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ED=5$\" width=\"63\" height=\"12\" /> and line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/2/bc2afec04e3636c80fa3d89e5468045595151c4d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ED$\" width=\"29\" height=\"12\" /> is perpendicular to line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />. Segment <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/6/9a65c05bd85d2609bea5db2b109ed5f556463511.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AE$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" /> intersects the circle at a point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. What is the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle  ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/b/10be58f9a6e8a551c79e43512eb76a80d9021e23.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ \\frac{120}{37}\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ \\frac{140}{39}\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ \\frac{145}{39}\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{140}{37}\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\frac{120}{31}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"462\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/9/2d9d724a509668795b085a7dbebb4169868878cd.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy]  /* Geogebra to Asymptote conversion, documentation at artofproblemsolving.com/Wiki, go to User:Azjps/geogebra */ import graph; size(8.865514650638614cm);  real labelscalefactor = 0.5; /* changes label-to-point distance */ pen dps = linewidth(0.7) + fontsize(10); defaultpen(dps); /* default pen style */  pen dotstyle = black; /* point style */  real xmin = -6.36927122464312, xmax = 11.361758076634109, ymin = -3.789601803155515, ymax = 7.420015026296013;  /* image dimensions    draw((-2.,0.)--(0.6486486486486486,1.8918918918918919)--(2.,0.)--cycle);   /* draw figures */ draw(circle((0.,0.), 2.));  draw((-2.,0.)--(5.,5.));  draw((5.,5.)--(5.,0.));  draw((5.,0.)--(-2.,0.));  draw((-2.,0.)--(0.6486486486486486,1.8918918918918919));  draw((0.6486486486486486,1.8918918918918919)--(2.,0.));  draw((2.,0.)--(-2.,0.));  draw((2.,0.)--(5.,5.));  draw((0.,0.)--(5.,5.));   /* dots and labels */ dot((0.,0.),dotstyle);  label(&quot;$O$&quot;, (-0.10330578512396349,-0.39365890308038826), NE * labelscalefactor);  dot((-2.,0.),dotstyle);  label(&quot;$A$&quot;, (-2.2370398196844437,-0.42371149511645134), NE * labelscalefactor);  dot((2.,0.),dotstyle);  label(&quot;$B$&quot;, (2.045454545454548,-0.36360631104432517), NE * labelscalefactor);  dot((5.,0.),dotstyle);  label(&quot;$D$&quot;, (4.900450788880542,-0.42371149511645134), NE * labelscalefactor);  dot((5.,5.),dotstyle);  label(&quot;$E$&quot;, (5.06574004507889,5.15104432757325), NE * labelscalefactor);  dot((0.6486486486486486,1.8918918918918919),linewidth(3.pt) + dotstyle);  label(&quot;$C$&quot;, (0.48271975957926694,2.100706235912847), NE * labelscalefactor);  clip((xmin,ymin)--(xmin,ymax)--(xmax,ymax)--(xmax,ymin)--cycle);   /* end of picture */ [/asy]\" width=\"438\" height=\"428\" />\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the center of the circle. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc6863df7c804512ed37550df24c3c74ec372e18.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EC + CA = EA = \\sqrt{AD^2 + DE^2} = \\sqrt{(2+2+3)^2 + 5^2} = \\sqrt{74}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"499\" height=\"32\" />. However, by Power of a Point, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/d/10d61f5dcecb0881fb0149d790e5afa022495d2a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(EC)(EC + CA) = EO^2 - R^2 = (2+3)^2 + 5^2 - 2^2 = 25 + 25 - 4 = 46 \\implies EC = \\frac{46}{\\sqrt{74}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"702\" height=\"43\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/9/b794ec6fd9179a28db8556fc6cae100d3fdf89aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC = \\sqrt{74} - \\frac{46}{\\sqrt{74}} = \\frac{28}{\\sqrt{74}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"205\" height=\"42\" />. Now <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/f/d7f734ab039022f1d47214b98675b71e1a07461d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC = \\sqrt{AB^2 - AC^2} = \\sqrt{4^2 - \\frac{28^2}{74}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{16 \\cdot 74 - 28^2}{74}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1184 - 784}{74}} = \\frac{20}{\\sqrt{74}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"607\" height=\"46\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/8/c284f4efd1e65e369c2eff7c074b55f3974c6309.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ACB = 90^{\\circ}, [ABC] = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot BC \\cdot AC = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{20}{\\sqrt{74}} \\cdot \\frac{28}{\\sqrt{74}} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{140}{37}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"527\" height=\"48\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2:_Similar_triangles_with_Pythagorean\">Solution 2: Similar triangles with Pythagorean</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2: Similar triangles with Pythagorean\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is the diameter of the circle, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/2/0728bebb477a2636300c56851b6a6f9e382c1b83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ACB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" /> is a right angle, and therefore by AA similarity, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/0/ac0058b20924ee6a2b2d270cf4babc0062a2d55d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ACB \\sim \\triangle ADE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"145\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>Because of this, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/d/09db56460ddb57045257c491b9e6e5447c71f958.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{AC}{AD} = \\frac{AB}{AE} \\Longrightarrow \\frac{AC}{2+2+3} = \\frac{2+2}{\\sqrt{7^2 + 5^2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"301\" height=\"42\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/5/495ccf41bb0c350e534652f3d30e5d0aa39f6542.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC = \\frac{28}{\\sqrt{74}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"88\" height=\"42\" />.\n</p><p>Likewise, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/b/22b00f2f4b3ca1d01440438bcc5ad380f2bfb059.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{BC}{ED} = \\frac{AB}{AE} \\Longrightarrow \\frac{BC}{5} = \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{74}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"223\" height=\"42\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/c/cfc3ef6be3f2d01defa5c4b71380407ecfcb4206.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC = \\frac{20}{\\sqrt{74}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"89\" height=\"42\" />.\n</p><p>Thus the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/9/aa9086be2299344dd3d0090d5fae3c88b9eb666a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{28}{\\sqrt{74}} \\cdot \\frac{20}{\\sqrt{74}} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{140}{37}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"301\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2b:_Area_shortcut\">Solution 2b: Area shortcut</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2b: Area shortcut\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/6/9a65c05bd85d2609bea5db2b109ed5f556463511.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AE$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/8/ec8f8cb0ae09e32f94672f87644de131030cc872.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{74}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, the ratio of the sides is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/4/8142c9744d77d00f48fe415a9d975ff2751ad79b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\sqrt{74}}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"35\" height=\"41\" />, meaning the ratio of the areas is thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/c/fcc92ac2ea722da3299c301edac660483b2d669b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${(\\frac{\\sqrt{74}}{4})}^2 \\implies \\frac{74}{16} \\implies \\frac{37}{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"200\" height=\"45\" />. We then have the proportion <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/5/6d53d4eeb806e3d9c801c70c63def6340603e898.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\frac{5*7}{2}}{[ABC]}=\\frac{37}{8} \\implies 37*[ABC]=140 \\implies \\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{140}{37}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"419\" height=\"48\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3:_Similar_triangles_without_Pythagorean\">Solution 3: Similar triangles without Pythagorean</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3: Similar triangles without Pythagorean\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Or, use similar triangles all the way, dispense with Pythagorean, and go for minimal calculation:\n</p><p>Draw <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/b/b9bbeaf9f95b6cdd918ad7c0af861ec81c2ab69d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BF \\parallel ED$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"77\" height=\"19\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/6/9a65c05bd85d2609bea5db2b109ed5f556463511.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AE$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/9/ab91bd6bc65ed6310688160af4eea1fd61787a87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BF=5\\times\\frac{4}{7}=\\frac{20}{7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"146\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/7/747324da593cee3185b6b415f1f334f37adfa51a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[\\triangle ABF]=\\frac{1}{2} \\times 4 \\times \\frac{20}{7}=\\frac{40}{7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"226\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/d/34d8e605ff271b2d2d26f88fb4d51f13758b1728.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC:CB:CF=49:35:25$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"224\" height=\"13\" />.  (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/7/93738778feba1b27ee97bf04b5fe03737413a16d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7:5$\" width=\"32\" height=\"12\" /> ratio applied twice)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/0/610475c7bb5cfaeda8b1d3c377142d751fe36993.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[\\triangle ABC]=\\frac{49}{49+25}[\\triangle ABF]=\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{140}{37}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"327\" height=\"48\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Coordinate_Geometry.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Coordinate_Geometry)\">Solution 4 (Coordinate Geometry)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Coordinate Geometry)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> be at the origin <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/b/c4b86f54134d69cad8793688a431aa907aaf2698.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0, 0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> of a coordinate plane, with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> being located at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/3/1c333f997c9d25799492b15b1579d35627b20f9a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(4, 0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, etc.\n</p><p>We can find the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> by finding the the altitude from line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> to point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. Realize that this altitude is the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> coordinate of point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> on the coordinate plane, since the respective base of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> is on the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-axis.\n</p><p>Using the diagram in solution one, the equation for circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/6/906b2d1239178bb7e7b56be28a656e3dfb4902a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x-2)^2+y^2 = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"136\" height=\"19\" />. \n</p><p>The equation for line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/6/9a65c05bd85d2609bea5db2b109ed5f556463511.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AE$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" /> is then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/6/ee663c0f40eeba009ecfb222cf742d06a354b0ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = \\frac{5}{7}x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"57\" height=\"37\" />, therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/c/2bc9ae6aef8cb5cbba37f98914c6dd45fc2fac09.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\frac{7}{5}y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"56\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p>Substituting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/7/c57f7ecc4590593a09082888162ed21032322d8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{7}{5}y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"22\" height=\"37\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> in the equation for circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, we get:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/6/aa6092974bb38a5748669539eb6af0af6c467a62.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\frac{7}{5}y-2\\right)^2+y^2 = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"162\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>We can solve for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> to yield the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> coordinate of point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> in the coordinate plane, since this is the point of intersection of the circle and line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/6/9a65c05bd85d2609bea5db2b109ed5f556463511.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AE$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" />. Note that one root will yield the intersection of the circle and line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/6/9a65c05bd85d2609bea5db2b109ed5f556463511.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AE$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" /> at the origin, so we will ignore this root.\n</p><p>Expanding the expression and factoring, we get:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/0/1607eefe28a2d56eb485f4bdb89c9b905d05587b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\frac{49}{25}y^2-\\frac{28}{5}y+4\\right)+y^2 = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"223\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/0/da0cb38ec7c488a30f789ae02cbf66f46c7dc019.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{74}{25}y^2-\\frac{28}{5}y = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"126\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/6/c865b669adbb53dbab97d3a0382d93574a0a9ce2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$50y(37y-70) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"143\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Our non-zero root is thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/8/e8847d3a4d83d44a0aca19f14709f7bb87a8148b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{70}{37}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"20\" height=\"37\" />. Calculating the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> as the length of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/8/e8847d3a4d83d44a0aca19f14709f7bb87a8148b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{70}{37}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"20\" height=\"37\" /> as the altitude, we get:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/1/3a180fb738da3f19636114a0933e31c5bb15efa6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(4)(\\frac{70}{37})}{2} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{140}{37}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"161\" height=\"48\" />.\n</p><p>-Solution by Joeya\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28No_sqrts.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(No_sqrts)\">Solution 5 (No sqrts)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (No sqrts)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Slope of AC is 5/7\nAs stated in other solutions AB is the diameter, ABC is right.\n</p><p>Let CF be an altitude of ABC.\n</p><p>AF:CF = CF:BF = 7:5 \n</p><p>We can set AF = 49, CF = 35, BF = 25 and scale back later\n</p><p>Then the radius is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/f/e6ff57726a4208985251ff2a7cc1c547d1e6a29a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{AB}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"34\" height=\"39\" /> = <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/0/c40a76fe7e5f04301385a464a372a9a552a4c090.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{AF+BF}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"85\" height=\"39\" /> = <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/5/de5cebb3d62563f8831d634abf7200dce9f19d41.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{74}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"24\" height=\"38\" /> = <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/9/78916550838bd31d66e4cdefbc366edb11fd00bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$37$\" width=\"18\" height=\"12\" />. \n</p><p>So the radius is 37 and the height of ABC is 35.\n</p><p>If we scale it back so that our radius is 2, our height is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/8/e8847d3a4d83d44a0aca19f14709f7bb87a8148b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{70}{37}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"20\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p>Area of ABC is bh/2 = <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/2/312f7133b248efa20e573b22060c15082cae4fbe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(4)(\\frac{70}{37})}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"60\" height=\"42\" /> = <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/6/556f53a39dc7ed14e3f3ecf68ee8d9f0e31b64b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{140}{37}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"79\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>-mathophobia\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_6:_Trigonometry_.26_Polar_Coordinates\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6:_Trigonometry_&amp;_Polar_Coordinates\">Solution 6: Trigonometry &amp; Polar Coordinates</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6: Trigonometry &amp; Polar Coordinates\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We'll use the diagram from Solution <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the center of the circle at the origin. We know that through our knowledge of Polar Coordinates, point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> lies on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/3/c43df6241e7de6749c2901fcdca10f83c95d9aae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2\\cos(\\theta), 2\\sin(\\theta))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"141\" height=\"20\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52e8ed7a3ba22130ad3984eb2cd413406475a689.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is defined as the measure of central angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/d/e2d87c2746bf18130f641d2c6b17a0fa902f7863.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle COB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"58\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Let angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/8/0e8b52791afacd798fda4d9ab4ddbf3e5e52673f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle EAD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"58\" height=\"14\" /> have measure <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/f/10f32377ac67d94f764f12a15ea987e88c85d3e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\alpha$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" />. We can see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/1/db1c8ae14989939f9a7aea9342715c4e1d826220.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tan(\\alpha) = \\frac{5}{7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"91\" height=\"38\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/7/9e748bd853094fb23e4b1b0f7b9d07cdab433cc1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(\\alpha) = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{74}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"112\" height=\"43\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/f/cefb085e8eba6c7822dcbf371c6907fce56c58c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos(\\alpha) = \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{74}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"114\" height=\"43\" />.\n</p><p>By the Inscribed Angle Theorem, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/7/af7e01751b9a8d9d35e72d3db1ec11235258be29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta = 2\\alpha$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"56\" height=\"14\" />, so the coordinates of point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> can be expressed as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/a/0fa66ad5c3ff8d4dcd8b66047136feeff862a1f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2\\cos(2\\alpha), 2\\sin(2\\alpha)).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"170\" height=\"20\" /> Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is at the origin, diameter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/0/840e2b592390eb6ec918fa6f3292716ce170de66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AB}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> lies on the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/0/7a06334068417b1781d14e8b28af827907961eb3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"42\" height=\"16\" />. As such, triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />'s base is just diameter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/0/840e2b592390eb6ec918fa6f3292716ce170de66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AB}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and the height is the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />-coordinate of point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/2/1923ba0b6e0adf72e656ce546abf6aacbc28149d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\sin(2\\alpha)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"71\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>The area of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> is just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/4/2940e83991c0e6677cca2a9a58e4c8ddf17895f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 4 \\cdot 2\\sin(2\\alpha)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"120\" height=\"38\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/4/944c9ffb8bf91055fe79d82ac54b2579251ff2c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4\\sin(2\\alpha)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"71\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Using double angle identities, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/4/7145329799ca3b20fab708ed6318993a7d39e097.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(2\\alpha) = 2\\sin(\\alpha)\\cos(\\alpha) = \\frac{2 \\cdot (5 \\cdot 7)}{(\\sqrt{74})^2} = \\frac{35}{37}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"339\" height=\"47\" />\n</p><p>The area of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/4/ed4e511ec2b6766f48e7c9d0764fa8815968618b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4 \\cdot 35}{37} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}~ \\frac{140}{37}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"148\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~Fernat123\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn_.28Similar_Triangles.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn_(Similar_Triangles)\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn (Similar Triangles)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by OmegaLearn (Similar Triangles)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/NsQbhYfGh1Q?t=512\">https://youtu.be/NsQbhYfGh1Q?t=512</a>\n</p><p>~ pi_is_3.14\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_12B\" title=\"2017 AMC 12B\">2017 AMC 12B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_12B_Problems\" title=\"2017 AMC 12B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_12B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2017 AMC 12B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_17\" title=\"2017 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 17\">Problem 17</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_19\" title=\"2017 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 19\">Pro